comments.body:
@bethehand 
Thanks for the measurement. One correction for the comparison plan: my forecast B (1.935) is built only on the scaling-ladder rows, which were evaluated at 4K packing, so it is a 4K-built forecast like the Aug 19 one. Forecast A (1.979) is anchored on the hero's 16 evals at 16K. This means my registered H1 and H4, scored against the 16K final value, favour my forecasts by about 0.03 from the packing change alone. I will score H1 to H4 as registered and report packing-corrected versions next to them: 4K-built forecasts against Y + Δ, with Δ from a 4K eval of the final checkpoint if one is logged, otherwise your 0.0306 (0.0283 as a sensitivity check). [This is fixed now, before the outcome](https://github.com/mindscanlabs/NAI-track-record/blob/main/forecasts/marin-535b.md#notes-added-before-the-outcome-25-october-2026).

On the 262K phase: +1 for keeping all 16 Paloma subsets (GrugEvalConfig.max_seq_len = 65,536 per #9890, or masking zero-token tags in the top-level macro). A 14-subset final value would make every forecast in this thread incomparable. A single 4K eval of the final checkpoint would also let the 4K-built forecasts be checked directly.
